{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go && nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go'","aggregated_output":"     1\tpackage pose\n     2\t\n     3\timport (\n     4\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n     5\t\t\"time\"\n     6\t)\n     7\t\n     8\t// The review run shim (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes,\n     9\t// review rounds 6 and 8): a descendant of the reviewer that leaves the\n    10\t// adapter's process group, starts its own session and clears its environment\n    11\t// matches neither the group kill nor the sealed-checkout entry. Making the\n    12\t// dispatcher a child subreaper caught it, but a subreaper is process-wide:\n    13\t// in the dispatching process it also adopted and ended children other code\n    14\t// started there (a detached delegation), and reaped processes another\n    15\t// goroutine was waiting for. The dispatcher therefore starts the adapter\n    16\t// through a shim, the same executable re-entered with ReviewShimArg, which\n    17\t// makes itself the subreaper, runs the adapter, and, once it exits, ends\n    18\t// every process left in its subtree. The shim has no other children, so\n    19\t// everything it adopts is the run's. A timeout asks the shim to stop with\n    20\t// SIGTERM, and the death of the dispatcher delivers it the same signal: in\n    21\t// both cases it ends the subtree before it exits (review round 8, N3, N4).\n    22\t\n    23\t// reviewReapWait bounds how long ending the run's leftover processes takes.\n    24\tconst reviewReapWait = 5 * time.Second\n    25\t\n    26\t// ReviewShimArg is the first argument that runs the executable as a review\n    27\t// run shim instead of the CLI.\n    28\tconst ReviewShimArg = \"__pose-review-run-shim\"\n    29\t\n    30\t// ReviewShimReportEnv tells the shim that the dispatcher passed it a report\n    31\t// pipe as file descriptor 3, and names the dispatcher's pid. The report never\n    32\t// goes to a path the reviewer can write (review round 8, N2).\n    33\tconst ReviewShimReportEnv = \"POSE_REVIEW_SHIM_REPORT\"\n    34\t\n    35\t// ReviewShimAvailable says the running executable handles ReviewShimArg. It\n    36\t// is set by the pose command and by the test binaries that dispatch; any\n    37\t// other embedder starts the adapter directly, without the shim.\n    38\tvar ReviewShimAvailable = false\n    39\t\n    40\t// reviewShimReport is what the shim found after the adapter exited.\n    41\ttype reviewShimReport struct {\n    42\t\tEnded      []string `json:\"ended\"`\n    43\t\tEndedTotal int      `json:\"ended_total\"`\n    44\t\tError      string   `json:\"error,omitempty\"`\n    45\t}\n    46\t\n    47\t// reviewShimReportMaxPids bounds the pids a report lists; EndedTotal counts\n    48\t// them all. A full report stays far below the dispatcher's read bound.\n    49\tconst reviewShimReportMaxPids = 1000\n    50\t\n    51\t// boundedReviewShimReport encodes r within the dispatcher's read bound, so\n    52\t// the dispatcher always reads it whole (review round 9, N2).\n    53\tfunc boundedReviewShimReport(r reviewShimReport) []byte {\n    54\t\tr.EndedTotal = len(r.Ended)\n    55\t\tif len(r.Ended) > reviewShimReportMaxPids {\n    56\t\t\tr.Ended = r.Ended[:reviewShimReportMaxPids]\n    57\t\t}\n    58\t\tif len(r.Error) > 4096 {\n    59\t\t\tr.Error = r.Error[:4096]\n    60\t\t}\n    61\t\traw, _ := json.Marshal(r)\n    62\t\treturn raw\n    63\t}\n     1\t//go:build linux\n     2\t\n     3\tpackage pose\n     4\t\n     5\timport (\n     6\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n     7\t\t\"errors\"\n     8\t\t\"fmt\"\n     9\t\t\"os\"\n    10\t\t\"os/exec\"\n    11\t\t\"os/signal\"\n    12\t\t\"strconv\"\n    13\t\t\"strings\"\n    14\t\t\"syscall\"\n    15\t\t\"time\"\n    16\t\n    17\t\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n    18\t)\n    19\t\n    20\t// RunReviewShim runs args (the adapter's path, then its argv) as the shim's\n    21\t// only child, waits for it or for SIGTERM, ends every process left in the\n    22\t// shim's subtree and returns the adapter's exit status.\n    23\tfunc RunReviewShim(args []string) int {\n    24\t\treport := reviewShimReport{}\n    25\t\tvar reportFile *os.File\n    26\t\tparent := 0\n    27\t\tif value := os.Getenv(ReviewShimReportEnv); value != \"\" {\n    28\t\t\tparent, _ = strconv.Atoi(value)\n    29\t\t\t// The report pipe is the shim's alone: the adapter never inherits it.\n    30\t\t\tsyscall.CloseOnExec(3)\n    31\t\t\treportFile = os.NewFile(3, \"pose-review-shim-report\")\n    32\t\t}\n    33\t\t_ = os.Unsetenv(ReviewShimReportEnv)\n    34\t\twrite := func() {\n    35\t\t\tif reportFile == nil {\n    36\t\t\t\treturn\n    37\t\t\t}\n    38\t\t\traw := boundedReviewShimReport(report)\n    39\t\t\t_, _ = reportFile.Write(raw)\n    40\t\t\t_ = reportFile.Close()\n    41\t\t}\n    42\t\tif len(args) < 2 {\n    43\t\t\treport.Error = \"the review shim was given no adapter\"\n    44\t\t\twrite()\n    45\t\t\treturn 125\n    46\t\t}\n    47\t\tstop := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n    48\t\tsignal.Notify(stop, syscall.SIGTERM)\n    49\t\t// The dispatcher's death stops the shim the same way as a timeout.\n    50\t\tif err := unix.Prctl(unix.PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, uintptr(syscall.SIGTERM), 0, 0, 0); err != nil {\n    51\t\t\treport.Error = \"the shim could not follow its dispatcher: \" + err.Error()\n    52\t\t\twrite()\n    53\t\t\treturn 125\n    54\t\t}\n    55\t\tif parent != 0 && os.Getppid() != parent {\n    56\t\t\treport.Error = \"the dispatcher ended before the shim started\"\n    57\t\t\twrite()\n    58\t\t\treturn 125\n    59\t\t}\n    60\t\tif err := unix.Prctl(unix.PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER, 1, 0, 0, 0); err != nil {\n    61\t\t\treport.Error = \"the shim could not become the subreaper of the run: \" + err.Error()\n    62\t\t\twrite()\n    63\t\t\treturn 125\n    64\t\t}\n    65\t\tcmd := &exec.Cmd{Path: args[0], Args: args[1:], Stdin: os.Stdin, Stdout: os.Stdout, Stderr: os.Stderr, Env: os.Environ()}\n    66\t\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n    67\t\t\treport.Error = \"the adapter could not run: \" + err.Error()\n    68\t\t\twrite()\n    69\t\t\treturn 127\n    70\t\t}\n    71\t\tdone := make(chan error, 1)\n    72\t\tgo func() { done <- cmd.Wait() }()\n    73\t\tcode, stopped := 0, false\n    74\t\tvar signaled syscall.Signal\n    75\t\tselect {\n    76\t\tcase err := <-done:\n    77\t\t\tvar exit *exec.ExitError\n    78\t\t\tif errors.As(err, &exit) {\n    79\t\t\t\tif status, ok := exit.Sys().(syscall.WaitStatus); ok && status.Signaled() {\n    80\t\t\t\t\tsignaled, code = status.Signal(), 128+int(status.Signal())\n    81\t\t\t\t} else {\n    82\t\t\t\t\tcode = exit.ExitCode()\n    83\t\t\t\t}\n    84\t\t\t} else if err != nil {\n    85\t\t\t\treport.Error = \"the adapter's end could not be read: \" + err.Error()\n    86\t\t\t}\n    87\t\tcase <-stop:\n    88\t\t\tstopped, code = true, 128+int(syscall.SIGTERM)\n    89\t\t}\n    90\t\tended, err := reapShimSubtree()\n    91\t\treport.Ended = ended\n    92\t\tif err != nil && report.Error == \"\" {\n    93\t\t\treport.Error = err.Error()\n    94\t\t}\n    95\t\twrite()\n    96\t\tif stopped {\n    97\t\t\treturn code\n    98\t\t}\n    99\t\t// An adapter ended by a signal is reported as ended by it: the shim ends\n   100\t\t// itself the same way, so the dispatcher reads what it would have read\n   101\t\t// without the shim.\n   102\t\tif signaled != 0 {\n   103\t\t\tsignal.Reset(signaled)\n   104\t\t\t_ = syscall.Kill(os.Getpid(), signaled)\n   105\t\t\ttime.Sleep(time.Second)\n   106\t\t}\n   107\t\treturn code\n   108\t}\n   109\t\n   110\t// stopReviewShim asks a running shim to end its subtree and exit.\n   111\tfunc stopReviewShim(cmd *exec.Cmd) func() error {\n   112\t\treturn func() error { return cmd.Process.Signal(syscall.SIGTERM) }\n   113\t}\n   114\t\n   115\t// reapShimSubtree ends and reaps every child of the shim, including the\n   116\t// orphans its subreaper status adopted, until none is left.\n   117\tfunc reapShimSubtree() ([]string, error) {\n   118\t\tself := os.Getpid()\n   119\t\tvar ended []string\n   120\t\tseen := map[int]bool{}\n   121\t\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(reviewReapWait)\n   122\t\t// A process that cannot be inspected or ended is not a reason to stop:\n   123\t\t// the others are still ended, and the problem is reported at the end\n   124\t\t// (review round 9, N3).\n   125\t\tvar problems []string\n   126\t\tnote := func(err error) {\n   127\t\t\tif len(problems) < 8 {\n   128\t\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, err.Error())\n   129\t\t\t}\n   130\t\t}\n   131\t\tfor {\n   132\t\t\tchildren := 0\n   133\t\t\tproblems = problems[:0]\n   134\t\t\t// /proc is read in batches under the deadline, never materialized\n   135\t\t\t// whole (review round 13, N3).\n   136\t\t\tdirs, err := readProcBatched(deadline)\n   137\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   138\t\t\t\tnote(err)\n   139\t\t\t}\n   140\t\t\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n   141\t\t\t\tpid, err := strconv.Atoi(d.Name())\n   142\t\t\t\tif err != nil || pid == self {\n   143\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   144\t\t\t\t}\n   145\t\t\t\tst, err := readShimProcStat(pid)\n   146\t\t\t\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) {\n   147\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   148\t\t\t\t}\n   149\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   150\t\t\t\t\t// A process of another user is never the run's: with procfs\n   151\t\t\t\t\t// mounted hidepid it is visible and unreadable (review round 10,\n   152\t\t\t\t\t// N1). Only one of this user is an unknown that may be the run's.\n   153\t\t\t\t\tif owner, oerr := reviewProcessOwner(pid); reviewProcessGoneErr(oerr) || oerr == nil && owner != os.Getuid() {\n   154\t\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   155\t\t\t\t\t}\n   156\t\t\t\t\tchildren++\n   157\t\t\t\t\tnote(fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be inspected: %w\", pid, err))\n   158\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   159\t\t\t\t}\n   160\t\t\t\tif st.ppid != self {\n   161\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   162\t\t\t\t}\n   163\t\t\t\tchildren++\n   164\t\t\t\tif !st.zombie {\n   165\t\t\t\t\t// A pid is reported ended only once its signal was delivered\n   166\t\t\t\t\t// (review round 10, N3).\n   167\t\t\t\t\tif delivered, err := killPinnedChild(pid, self); err != nil {\n   168\t\t\t\t\t\tnote(err)\n   169\t\t\t\t\t} else if delivered {\n   170\t\t\t\t\t\tseen[pid] = true\n   171\t\t\t\t\t}\n   172\t\t\t\t}\n   173\t\t\t}\n   174\t\t\tfor {\n   175\t\t\t\tpid, err := unix.Wait4(-1, nil, unix.WNOHANG, nil)\n   176\t\t\t\tif pid <= 0 || err != nil {\n   177\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n   178\t\t\t\t}\n   179\t\t\t\t// A pid is reported ended once it was signalled and reaped\n   180\t\t\t\t// (review round 12, N2).\n   181\t\t\t\tif seen[pid] {\n   182\t\t\t\t\tdelete(seen, pid)\n   183\t\t\t\t\tended = append(ended, strconv.Itoa(pid))\n   184\t\t\t\t}\n   185\t\t\t}\n   186\t\t\tif children == 0 && len(problems) == 0 {\n   187\t\t\t\treturn ended, nil\n   188\t\t\t}\n   189\t\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n   190\t\t\t\treturn ended, fmt.Errorf(\"%d process(es) of the run could not be ended: %s\", children, strings.Join(problems, \"; \"))\n   191\t\t\t}\n   192\t\t\ttime.Sleep(20 * time.Millisecond)\n   193\t\t}\n   194\t}\n   195\t\n   196\ttype shimProcStat struct {\n   197\t\tppid   int\n   198\t\tzombie bool\n   199\t}\n   200\t\n   201\tfunc readShimProcStat(pid int) (shimProcStat, error) {\n   202\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\")\n   203\t\tif err != nil {\n   204\t\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, err\n   205\t\t}\n   206\t\ttext := string(raw)\n   207\t\tend := strings.LastIndexByte(text, ')')\n   208\t\tif end < 0 {\n   209\t\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n   210\t\t}\n   211\t\tf := strings.Fields(text[end+1:])\n   212\t\tif len(f) < 2 {\n   213\t\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n   214\t\t}\n   215\t\tppid, err := strconv.Atoi(f[1])\n   216\t\tif err != nil {\n   217\t\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n   218\t\t}\n   219\t\treturn shimProcStat{ppid: ppid, zombie: f[0] == \"Z\"}, nil\n   220\t}\n   221\t\n   222\t// killPinnedChild signals pid through a pidfd while it is still a child of\n   223\t// parent: a pid reused by an unrelated process is never signalled. It says\n   224\t// whether the signal reached the process; one that already ended was not\n   225\t// ended by the shim and is not reported (review round 11, N2).\n   226\tfunc killPinnedChild(pid, parent int) (bool, error) {\n   227\t\tfd, err := unix.PidfdOpen(pid, 0)\n   228\t\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n   229\t\t\treturn false, nil\n   230\t\t}\n   231\t\tif err != nil {\n   232\t\t\treturn false, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be pinned: %w\", pid, err)\n   233\t\t}\n   234\t\tdefer unix.Close(fd)\n   235\t\tst, err := readShimProcStat(pid)\n   236\t\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) || err == nil && (st.ppid != parent || st.zombie) {\n   237\t\t\treturn false, nil\n   238\t\t}\n   239\t\tif err != nil {\n   240\t\t\treturn false, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be inspected: %w\", pid, err)\n   241\t\t}\n   242\t\tif err := unix.PidfdSendSignal(fd, unix.SIGKILL, nil, 0); err != nil {\n   243\t\t\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n   244\t\t\t\treturn false, nil\n   245\t\t\t}\n   246\t\t\treturn false, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be ended: %w\", pid, err)\n   247\t\t}\n   248\t\treturn true, nil\n   249\t}\n   250\t\n   251\t// parseReviewShimReport reads the report the shim wrote to its pipe.\n   252\tfunc parseReviewShimReport(raw []byte) (reviewShimReport, error) {\n   253\t\tvar r reviewShimReport\n   254\t\tif len(raw) == 0 {\n   255\t\t\treturn r, fmt.Errorf(\"the review shim left no report\")\n   256\t\t}\n   257\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &r); err != nil {\n   258\t\t\treturn r, fmt.Errorf(\"the review shim report is unreadable: %w\", err)\n   259\t\t}\n   260\t\treturn r, nil\n   261\t}\n   262\t\n   263\t// readProcBatched lists the numeric entries of /proc in batches, and stops\n   264\t// with an error once deadline passes.\n   265\tfunc readProcBatched(deadline time.Time) ([]os.DirEntry, error) {\n   266\t\tf, err := os.Open(\"/proc\")\n   267\t\tif err != nil {\n   268\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"the run's processes could not be listed: %w\", err)\n   269\t\t}\n   270\t\tdefer f.Close()\n   271\t\tvar out []os.DirEntry\n   272\t\tfor {\n   273\t\t\tbatch, err := f.ReadDir(512)\n   274\t\t\tfor _, d := range batch {\n   275\t\t\t\tif d.Name()[0] >= '0' && d.Name()[0] <= '9' {\n   276\t\t\t\t\tout = append(out, d)\n   277\t\t\t\t}\n   278\t\t\t}\n   279\t\t\tif err != nil || len(batch) == 0 {\n   280\t\t\t\treturn out, nil\n   281\t\t\t}\n   282\t\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n   283\t\t\t\treturn out, fmt.Errorf(\"the run's processes could not be listed within the bound\")\n   284\t\t\t}\n   285\t\t}\n   286\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md | sed -n '105,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"   105\t\n   106\t### Technical risks\n   107\tOn Linux the pose command runs the adapter under the review run shim, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree and ends what the run leaves in it after the adapter exits, on a timeout and when the dispatcher ends; the subtree is the run's by construction. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout environment entry, which a reviewer process can clear or set (L6). Outside Linux only the process-group kill applies (L2).\n   108\t\n   109\t## 4. Tasks\n   110\t\n   111\t### Planning\n   112\t- [ ] Confirm the cause against the current engine before starting.\n   113\t\n   114\t### Implementation\n   115\t- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n   116\t\n   117\t### Validation\n   118\t- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## 5. Decisions\n   121\t\n   122\t### Decision D1\n   123\t- Basis: R1, R2\n   124\tThe run's leftovers are identified by the process tree, not by the environment: on Linux the adapter runs under a shim that is the subreaper of the run's subtree, so every process the run starts, including one that opens its own session or clears its environment, stays the shim's to end. A subreaper in the dispatcher itself was rejected because it is process-wide there and adopted children other code started in the same process. The sealed-checkout entry the dispatch already sets remains a fallback for an embedder without the shim.\n   125\t\n   126\t### Decision D2\n   127\t- Basis: R5\n   128\tAn abandoned attempt is closed by the records a failed run writes (a run record and its receipt), released only when its marker is stalled and its owner proven dead. Nothing is deleted from the ledger, and older engines read the result as an ordinary failed run.\n   129\t\n   130\t## 6. Validation\n   131\t\n   132\t### Strategy\n   133\tEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n   134\t\n   135\t### Deterministic checks\n   136\t\n   137\t#### Test\n   138\t- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n   139\t\n   140\t### Execution log\n   141\t- 2026-11-24: implemented R1–R6. Each has a test that fails with its fix mutated out: the dispatch ends a process the adapter left in a session of its own (the test's leftover keeps writing into the runtime directory), the release records the failed run and receipt and removes the marker and its stall claim, a live owner is never released, and `review-brief/10` asks commands to finish while `/9` stays unchanged.\n   142\t- 2026-11-24: the containment record disclosed escaped-descendant termination as `unavailable`, and a regression test pinned that limitation. On Linux it is now `environment-marked` (ended while the descendant keeps the run's sealed-checkout entry, never `available`, so a profile that requires the full guarantee is still refused), the limitation text says so, and the test now proves the escaped child is ended with the run.\n   143\t- 2026-11-24: review round 1 (rrn-c5c082703d83c834) requested changes. N1: the reservation was published before the active marker, so a dispatch ended in between left an attempt nothing could release; the dispatch now marks the attempt active first and publishes the reservation after, and a stalled marker without a reservation is removed by the release. When marking itself fails, the dispatch still publishes the reservation and records the failed run and its receipt, keeping the contract of `pose-delegation-result-before-marker`. N2: `delegate ls` now names the abandonment cause and the release command, in text and JSON. N3: each process is pinned by a pidfd and its environment read again through it before SIGKILL, so a reused pid is never signalled. N4: a scan that cannot list `/proc` or read a process state fails; a process of this user whose environment cannot be read is counted and disclosed in the run record rather than skipped (L3). N5: the manual says the reaping is Linux-only. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out (N4's disclosure is not reproducible in a test: it needs a non-inspectable process).\n   144\t- 2026-11-24: review round 2 (rrn-2c9fa8ac47c2fbab) requested changes. N1: a reservation an earlier engine left without any marker is now listed by `delegate ls` and released by `cancel` once it has no marker, receipt or run and is older than ten minutes (an earlier engine marked within moments of reserving). N2: a non-inspectable process of this user that did not exist before the adapter started may be the run's, so the scan fails and the run stays unconcludable; ones that predate the run are skipped and disclosed. N3: a release claims the attempt exclusively, so two cancels never record two runs. N4: `TestAnInterruptedDispatchIsReleasedAndTheNextOneRuns` interrupts a real dispatch after its reservation, sees the next dispatch refused with the release command, releases it and dispatches again. N5: a receipt that cannot be inspected is reported with its error. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out, except N5.\n   145\t- 2026-11-24: review round 3 (rrn-667b69e8c01c345c) requested changes. N1: the release claim records its owner; a claim whose owner is gone is taken over, and a release interrupted after recording its run completes with that run instead of recording another. N2: age proves nothing about a markerless reservation, so it is released only with `--confirm-abandoned`, the operator's assertion that no dispatch of it runs, which the recorded run states. N3: reservations and receipts that cannot be read are reported as losses by `delegate ls`. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n   146\t- 2026-11-24: review round 4 (rrn-97acfcc2809e32c2) requested changes. N1: `pose delegate cancel` now asks the release for every attempt that is not running, so a release interrupted after recording its run is completed even when the attempt already shows that run; an abandonment run no longer counts as the attempt's run when looking for orphans. N2: a stale claim is taken over by renaming it, which only one taker wins, then an exclusive publish. N3: with the run history incomplete, the release refuses to rule a run out and the listing reports why. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n   147\t- 2026-11-24: review round 5 (rrn-146bc70da7364cde) requested changes. N1: the published claim and its takeover protocol are replaced by an operating-system lock (`flock`) on a per-attempt lock file, which the kernel releases when its holder ends, so there is nothing to take over and no window between two takers. N2: a release that finds the receipt already published and the marker still stalled completes by removing the marker. N3: the release completes with any run the attempt already recorded (by its dispatch or by an earlier release), never writing a second one. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n   148\t- 2026-11-24: review round 6 (rrn-adf91bb46841bd66) requested changes. N1: an unreadable active marker may be a live attempt's, so no reservation is then taken as markerless. N2: a descendant that leaves the group, starts its own session and clears its environment is now ended too on Linux: the dispatcher makes itself a child subreaper, and after the adapter exits it ends every child reparented to it in another session, started after the adapter, that does not name another run's checkout, pinning each by pidfd and reaping it. N3: the disclosed count holds only processes that predate the run. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n   149\t- 2026-11-24: review round 7 (rrn-9abc2fe5dedc7478) requested changes. N1, N2: a subreaper that cannot be enabled or a run start that cannot be read now fails the cleanup instead of skipping the orphan scan. N3: a process start that cannot be read, in the baseline or the orphan scan, is an error, and a process absent from the baseline is never taken as preexisting. N4: the run record names the ended processes by pid only; their names, which the reviewer chooses, are not recorded. N4 has a test; N1–N3 are error paths the host cannot be made to produce in a test.\n   150\t- 2026-11-24: the matrix of round 8 failed `TestReviewDispatchRunsAndRecordsAdapters`, and the cause was the design, not the test: a subreaper is process-wide, so the dispatcher also adopted and ended children other code started in the same process (a detached delegation) and could reap a process another goroutine waited for. The subreaper moved into a review run shim: the dispatcher starts the adapter as `<pose> __pose-review-run-shim <adapter path> <argv>`, the shim makes itself the subreaper, runs the adapter as its only child, ends and reaps everything left in its subtree, reports the ended pids in a file the dispatcher reads, and returns the adapter's status, re-raising its signal so a lost process still reads as lost. The shim has no other children, so all it adopts is the run's. The pose command and the test binaries that dispatch enable it; another embedder starts the adapter directly. `TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded` fails with the shim's reaping removed.\n   151\t- 2026-11-24: review round 8 (rrn-f19179878d867e9a) requested changes. N1: the environment entry is the reviewer's to set, so with the shim the dispatcher no longer searches by it; the shim's subtree is the run's by construction, and the search remains only for an embedder without the shim (L6). N2: the shim's report goes through a pipe passed as file descriptor 3, which the shim closes on exec, never through a path the reviewer can write. N3: a timeout sends the shim SIGTERM, and the shim ends the run's subtree before it exits; the group kill after the run stays the backstop. N4: the shim receives SIGTERM when its dispatcher ends (`PR_SET_PDEATHSIG`, checked against the dispatcher's pid at start) and ends the run, so a dead dispatcher never leaves a reviewer running. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n   152\t- 2026-11-24: review round 9 (rrn-5bbd458c7701af31) requested changes. N1 (follow-up): the manual and the recorded limitation now describe the shim as the Linux path and the environment search as the fallback of an embedder without it. N2: the shim's report is bounded (at most 1000 pids, with the total counted, and a bounded error) and the dispatcher drains whatever exceeds its read, so a large report never blocks the shim. N3: a process the shim cannot inspect or end no longer stops the reaping of the others; the problems are reported when the bound expires. N1 and N2 have tests that fail with them mutated out; N3 needs an uninspectable child, which the test host cannot produce.\n   153\t- 2026-11-24: review round 10 (rrn-ecfc61a1c2b009f6) requested changes. N1: the shim no longer counts a process of another user whose stat it cannot read (procfs mounted hidepid) as a possible run child; only an unreadable process of this user is. N2 (follow-up): the orphan scan lists each directory with a bound and reports a cut listing as a loss. N3 (follow-up): the shim reports a pid only once its signal was delivered, and its error is recorded in the run's limitations. N4 (follow-up): Technical risks, D1 and the fallback helper's comment describe the shim as the Linux path. N2 has a test; N1 and N3 need a hidepid procfs or an unkillable child.\n   154\t- 2026-11-25: round 11 first hit the Codex usage limit; its resume pointed the reviewer at the removed checkout of the first attempt, so a new run reviewed the same bundle (rrn-2b8c0f5ecc1b6196) and requested changes. N1: a shim that cannot be set up now fails the run instead of falling back to the environment search. N2 (follow-up): a child that already ended is not reported as ended by the shim. N3 (follow-up): `cancel` says when it completed an attempt with a run already recorded, with that run's status. N2 has a test that fails with it mutated out; N1 needs `os.Executable` or `pipe` to fail, and N3 is a message of the CLI branch stated untested in Known gaps.\n   155\t- 2026-11-25: review round 12 (rrn-1b7f5566a0a0e47e) requested changes. N1: the manual and the run record no longer claim a process-group kill on Windows, where the dispatch never ended the adapter's descendants; the limit is L2, and a containment profile that requires `process-group-termination` was and stays refused there. N2 (follow-up): the shim reports a pid ended only after it was signalled and reaped. N3 (follow-up): the recorded count is the shim's total, not the listed pids plus a placeholder.\n   156\t- 2026-11-25: review round 13 (rrn-0d9d3f3755d48f99) kept Windows as a blocker: R1 has no OS exception. By the maintainer's decision the Windows dispatch now ends the reviewer's tree: the adapter starts suspended, joins a job object limited to kill on close, and only then resumes, so no descendant starts outside it; the dispatch terminates the job after the run and on a timeout, and the kill-on-close limit ends the tree if the dispatcher dies. N2 (follow-up) stays declared as L6, the fallback of an embedder without the shim. N3 (follow-up): the shim reads /proc in batches under its deadline. The Windows test compiles for Windows but cannot run on this Linux host.\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\t### Results summary\n   160\tThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\t### Requirement trace\n   164\t- R1 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning, test:TestATimeoutEndsTheRunsOrphans, test:TestTheShimEndsTheRunWhenItsDispatcherEnds, test:TestTheAdapterDoesNotInheritTheShimReport, test:TestTheShimReportIsBounded, test:TestAChildThatAlreadyEndedIsNotReportedEnded, test:TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded, test:TestAPidThatLostTheRunsEntryIsNotKilled, test:TestANonInspectableProcessStartedDuringTheRunFailsTheScan\n   165\t- R2 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning\n   166\t- R3 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefAsksCommandsToFinish\n   167\t- R4 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning\n   168\t- R5 [satisfied] test:TestAnAbandonedAttemptIsReleasedWithARecord, test:TestTheAttemptIsMarkedActiveBeforeItIsReserved, test:TestAStalledMarkerWithoutReservationIsListedAndRemoved\n   169\t- R6 [satisfied] test:TestAnInterruptedDispatchIsReleasedAndTheNextOneRuns, test:TestAMarkerlessReservationOfAnEarlierEngineIsReleased, test:TestOneReleaseRunsAtATime, test:TestARecordedRunCompletesItsAttempt, test:TestAnInterruptedReleaseCompletesWithItsRun, test:TestAnUnreadableReservationIsReportedAsALoss, test:TestAnIncompleteRunHistoryRulesNothingOut, test:TestAnUnreadableMarkerRulesNothingOut, test:TestTheOrphanScanListsBoundedDirectories\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\t### Known gaps\n   173\tThe release is driven through the Store, including after a real interrupted dispatch; the thin `pose delegate cancel` branch that calls it is not driven through the CLI, because a stalled marker there needs a dispatch process killed and three silent heartbeat intervals. N5 (the receipt inspection error) has no test: making the receipt path uninspectable also makes the reservation unreadable first.\n   174\t\n   175\t\n   176\t## 7. Final Report\n   177\t\n   178\t### Delivered scope\n   179\tThe dispatch ends the processes a run left running before its cleanup and names them in the run record; `pose delegate cancel` releases an attempt abandoned before its run was recorded; `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish.\n   180\t\n   181\t\n   182\t### Files and modules changed\n   183\t`pose-mcp` (review dispatch, attempt ledger, delegation CLI, brief), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n   184\t\n   185\t\n   186\t### Validation executed\n   187\t`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t### Residual risks\n   191\tL1: on Linux a run's process escapes only while it keeps a live parent outside the run and neither the run's group nor its environment entry, for example one handed to a service manager; the cleanup inventory still fails a run whose files it keeps changing. L2: on other Unix platforms only the process-group kill applies. On Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object; its test (`TestTheReviewersJobEndsItsDescendants`) is built for Windows and was compiled and vetted here, but not run, since this host is Linux. L6: an embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout entry, which a reviewer process can set. L4: a reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner, so its release rests on the operator's confirmation, not on proof. L3: a process of this user that made itself non-inspectable cannot be matched: one that predates the run is skipped and counted in the run record, and one started during the run fails the cleanup, so such a run is not concludable.\n   192\t\n   193\t\n   194\t### Follow-ups\n   195\tNone.\n   196\t\n   197\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
