{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i 'lingering reviewer|review run shim|reap(s|ing)|abandoned attempt|markerless reservation' .pose/knowledge .pose/specs 2>/dev/null | head -100\"","aggregated_output":".pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:2:slug: pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:13:delivers: capability:review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:16:# Spec: pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:21:Keep a delegated review run concludable when the reviewer ends while a process it started is still running, by terminating and reaping the run's remaining processes before the cleanup inventory.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:57:- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:95:- capability:review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate.go\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:107:On Linux the pose command runs the adapter under the review run shim, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree and ends what the run leaves in it after the adapter exits, on a timeout and when the dispatcher ends; the subtree is the run's by construction. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout environment entry, which a reviewer process can clear or set (L6). Outside Linux only the process-group kill applies (L2).\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:128:An abandoned attempt is closed by the records a failed run writes (a run record and its receipt), released only when its marker is stalled and its owner proven dead. Nothing is deleted from the ledger, and older engines read the result as an ordinary failed run.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:143:- 2026-11-24: review round 1 (rrn-c5c082703d83c834) requested changes. N1: the reservation was published before the active marker, so a dispatch ended in between left an attempt nothing could release; the dispatch now marks the attempt active first and publishes the reservation after, and a stalled marker without a reservation is removed by the release. When marking itself fails, the dispatch still publishes the reservation and records the failed run and its receipt, keeping the contract of `pose-delegation-result-before-marker`. N2: `delegate ls` now names the abandonment cause and the release command, in text and JSON. N3: each process is pinned by a pidfd and its environment read again through it before SIGKILL, so a reused pid is never signalled. N4: a scan that cannot list `/proc` or read a process state fails; a process of this user whose environment cannot be read is counted and disclosed in the run record rather than skipped (L3). N5: the manual says the reaping is Linux-only. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out (N4's disclosure is not reproducible in a test: it needs a non-inspectable process).\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:145:- 2026-11-24: review round 3 (rrn-667b69e8c01c345c) requested changes. N1: the release claim records its owner; a claim whose owner is gone is taken over, and a release interrupted after recording its run completes with that run instead of recording another. N2: age proves nothing about a markerless reservation, so it is released only with `--confirm-abandoned`, the operator's assertion that no dispatch of it runs, which the recorded run states. N3: reservations and receipts that cannot be read are reported as losses by `delegate ls`. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:148:- 2026-11-24: review round 6 (rrn-adf91bb46841bd66) requested changes. N1: an unreadable active marker may be a live attempt's, so no reservation is then taken as markerless. N2: a descendant that leaves the group, starts its own session and clears its environment is now ended too on Linux: the dispatcher makes itself a child subreaper, and after the adapter exits it ends every child reparented to it in another session, started after the adapter, that does not name another run's checkout, pinning each by pidfd and reaping it. N3: the disclosed count holds only processes that predate the run. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:150:- 2026-11-24: the matrix of round 8 failed `TestReviewDispatchRunsAndRecordsAdapters`, and the cause was the design, not the test: a subreaper is process-wide, so the dispatcher also adopted and ended children other code started in the same process (a detached delegation) and could reap a process another goroutine waited for. The subreaper moved into a review run shim: the dispatcher starts the adapter as `<pose> __pose-review-run-shim <adapter path> <argv>`, the shim makes itself the subreaper, runs the adapter as its only child, ends and reaps everything left in its subtree, reports the ended pids in a file the dispatcher reads, and returns the adapter's status, re-raising its signal so a lost process still reads as lost. The shim has no other children, so all it adopts is the run's. The pose command and the test binaries that dispatch enable it; another embedder starts the adapter directly. `TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded` fails with the shim's reaping removed.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md:152:- 2026-11-24: review round 9 (rrn-5bbd458c7701af31) requested changes. N1 (follow-up): the manual and the recorded limitation now describe the shim as the Linux path and the environment search as the fallback of an embedder without it. N2: the shim's report is bounded (at most 1000 pids, with the total counted, and a bounded error) and the dispatcher drains whatever exceeds its read, so a large report never blocks the shim. N3: a process the shim cannot inspect or end no longer stops the reaping of the others; the problems are reported when the bound expires. N1 and N2 have tests that fail with them mutated out; N3 needs an uninspectable child, which the test host cannot produce.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
